{"id": "27", "question": "how to fix a door that won't lock", "gt": "To fix a door that won't lock, first try to align the door latch with the hole in the strike plate. If the latch is misaligned, you can perform a lipstick test to see where the latch contacts the strike plate. If it contacts too high or too low, tighten all the door's hinge screws. You may also need to enlarge the latch hole in the jamb or move the strike plate. If this doesn't work, shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb, pry it up until it comes loose and remove it. Replace the door stop back on the jamb, set back 1/4 inch from its original location. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally.", "pred": "To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "time_sec": 12.321, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 70.66, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "28", "question": "how to fix a door that won't lock", "gt": "To fix a door that won't lock, you can try the following steps based on the given passages:\n1. Close the door and attempt to lock it.\n2. If it doesn't lock, open the door and use a chisel to tap and pry off the door stop on the striker side of the jamb. Remove the top door stop as well.\n3. Place the door stop back on the jamb, aligning it 1/4 inch from its original location using the distinct line left behind.\n4. Nail the side and top pieces of the door stop back on.\n5. If the latch still doesn't catch, tighten the hinges first.\n6. Enlarge the latch hole in the jamb if necessary.\n7. Enlarge the strike plate hole or move the strike plate if the latch doesn't align with it.\n8. Ensure all the door's hinge screws are tight.\nFollowing these steps should help fix the door lock issue.", "pred": "To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "time_sec": 12.541, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.71, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "30", "question": "how to fix a door that won't lock", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to fix a door that won't lock:\n\nPassage 1 provides tips on how to repair stuck or frozen door locks, keys that don’t work, and latches that don’t latch. However, it does not specifically address the issue of a door that won't lock.\n\nPassage 2 provides instructions on how to fix a door that won't shut or lock properly by removing the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and aligning it back on. This method might work for doors that won't lock due to a misaligned door stop.\n\nPassage 3 provides additional troubleshooting steps for when a door latch won't catch due to misalignment with the strike plate. This passage suggests enlarging the latch hole in the jamb, tightening the hinges, and moving the strike plate to align with the latch.\n\nIn summary, there is no definitive solution in the provided passages to fix a door that won't lock directly. However, these passages provide general tips and troubleshooting steps that may help identify and resolve related issues such as misaligned door stops or latches. If the problem persists after trying these methods, it may be best to consult a professional locksmith or carpenter.", "pred": "To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "time_sec": 12.444, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.51, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "31", "question": "how to fix a door that won't lock", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to fix a door that won't lock:\n\n1. Check the door stop: According to passage 2, if the door won't lock, try tapping the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb. Pry up on it until it comes loose and remove it. Use the chisel to remove the door stop on top as well. Then, place the door stop back on the jamb, setting it back 1/4 inch from its original location. This should allow the door to shut and lock normally.\n2. Adjust the latch hole: If the latch doesn't align with the hole in the strike plate, you may need to enlarge the latch hole in the jamb, as suggested in passage 3. Before doing so, tighten the hinges first. Additionally, you may need to enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate.\n\nIf the passages do not provide sufficient information to answer the question, the response would be: \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"", "pred": "To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "time_sec": 12.404, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.3, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "32", "question": "how to fix a door that won't lock", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a possible solution to fix a door that won't lock:\n\n1. Check if the door stops are properly aligned. If the door stops are not aligned, use a chisel to remove the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it up until it comes loose. Remove the door stop and use the chisel to remove the door stop on top as well. Place the door stop back on the jamb and set it back 1/4 inch from its original location. Nail the side and top piece of door stop back on.\n2. Check if the latch hole in the jamb is properly aligned with the strike plate. If the latch doesn't align with the hole in the strike plate, tighten the hinges first. Then, enlarge the strike plate hole. Move the strike plate if necessary.\n\nIf these steps do not solve the problem, it may be necessary to seek further assistance from a professional locksmith or carpenter.", "pred": "To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fix a door that won't lock, first shut the door and try to lock it. If it won't lock, tap the end of a chisel under the door stop on the striker side of the jamb and pry it loose. Remove the door stop, then set it back 1/4 inch from its original location on the jamb, using the distinct line in the paint, stain, or varnish as a guide for alignment. Nail the side and top piece of the door stop back on. The door should now shut and lock normally. Additionally, if the door latch won't catch, tighten the hinges first, then enlarge the strike plate hole and move the strike plate to align with the latch.", "time_sec": 12.457, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.41, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "33", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "To cook pollock, first preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper and place them on the baking sheet. Layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, which should take about 15 minutes. If desired, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet and return fish to the oven to allow cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "time_sec": 9.06, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 85.47, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "34", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "To cook pollock, preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet sprayed with butter flavored cooking spray. Optionally, layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish is cooked and flakes easily. If desired, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven for approximately 3 minutes to allow the cheese to melt.", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "time_sec": 8.803, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 83.19, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "35", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "To cook pollock, first, preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Then, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Next, place the fillets on a baking sheet and layer them with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish flakes easily. If desired, you can also add cheese on top of the pollock fillets and bake for an additional 3 minutes to melt the cheese.", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "time_sec": 8.866, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "36", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to cook pollock:\n\nPassage 1:\n\n* Spray a baking sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\n* Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n* Place the fillets on the baking sheet and layer them with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n* Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n\nPassage 2:\n\n* Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\n* Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n* Bake the fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n\nPassage 3:\n\n* Bake the pollock fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n* Turn off the oven and place two slices of cheese on top of each fillet.\n* Return the fish to the oven and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\n\nTherefore, to cook pollock, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n2. Place the fillets on a baking sheet or in the oven.\n3. Cook the pollock until it flakes easily, either by baking it in the oven or on the stovetop.\n4. Add cheese to the pollock if desired and return it to the oven to melt the cheese.", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "time_sec": 8.973, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.48, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "37", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can cook pollock:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C) (Passage 2).\n2. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper (Passage 1 and 2).\n3. Place the pollock fillets on a baking sheet lined with butter-flavored cooking spray (Passage 1).\n4. Add onion, green pepper, and tomato slices to the baking sheet (Passage 1).\n5. Bake the pollock fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily (Passage 1 and 2).\n6. To add cheesy flavor, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven for an additional 3 minutes (Passage 3).\n\nNote that Passage 3 does not provide any specific instructions for cooking the pollock, but rather suggests adding cheese to the existing dish.", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "time_sec": 8.982, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.35, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "38", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to cook pollock:\n\nPreheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet lined with parchment paper or aluminum foil. You can also add sliced onions, green peppers, and tomatoes on top of the fillets. Bake for 15 minutes or until the fish flakes easily with a fork. If desired, top with cheese and return to the oven for an additional 3 minutes or until the cheese is melted.\n\nTherefore, the answer is not \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"", "pred": "Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Spray a baking sheet with butter flavored cooking spray, season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, place on the baking sheet and layer with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices, then bake in a preheated oven until the fish has cooked and flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Optionally, turn the oven off, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, return the fish to the oven, and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.", "time_sec": 9.102, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.74, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "39", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, you can run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Be careful not to damage the dryer's interior. You can then apply 1 to 2 tsp. of nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Afterward, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. You can mix 1 tsp. of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. Some plastic may be extremely difficult to remove, in which case you can try heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peel it off. If not all plastic comes off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "time_sec": 17.221, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 86.38, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "40", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is a brief answer to the question:\n\nTo get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first, run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Next, apply nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rinse the area with a damp cloth and then mix liquid laundry soap with water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "time_sec": 17.035, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 68.57, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "42", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. (Passage 1)\n2. Heat the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun to make it easier to peel off. Once heated, use a cloth to peel off the plastic. (Passage 2)\n3. Soak a cloth in an acetone-based nail polish remover and use it to scrub the remaining plastic off of the element after leaving it on the element for 5 minutes. Then, wipe the coils with a clean, damp cloth and remove any remaining residue with a cloth dipped in a solution of warm water and dish soap. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide specific instructions on how to remove plastic from the drum of a clothes dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "time_sec": 17.025, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.71, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "43", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to get plastic out of a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n4. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic is still difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun before attempting to scrape it off. However, if the plastic cannot be removed completely, it may be necessary to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "time_sec": 17.167, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 79.44, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "44", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\n4. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic does not come off with this method, another option is to use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic until it becomes soft and pliable. Then, attempt to peel it off carefully. However, be aware that this method may not work, and in that case, replacing the drum or the entire dryer may be necessary. It is recommended to call a service technician for this type of repair.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question of how to get plastic out of clothes dryer is:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\n3. Apply nail polish remover to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Rinse with a damp cloth and soap solution.\n5. Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic if necessary.\n\nIf the plastic still does not come off, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer.", "pred": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, first run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic. Then, scrape the plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the plastic to dissolve it. After that, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again. Finally, rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. If the plastic is extremely difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun, then peeling it off. If not all of the plastic can be removed, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer, in which case it is recommended to call a service technician to complete the repair.", "time_sec": 17.03, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.02, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "46", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "To fold a quilt as described in the passage, follow these steps:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To fold a quilt as described in the passage, follow these steps:  \n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface.  \n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.  \n\nThis method, used in \"The Timepiece Quilt\" tutorial (which measures 56 inches by 70 inches), applies to any quilt size.", "time_sec": 13.953, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
