{"id": "73", "question": "how to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are some ways to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you:\n\n1. Physical contact: Avoid physical contact that could be misinterpreted as fondling or sexual activity (Passage 1).\n2. Eye contact: If your coworker is consistently staring at you, it may be a sign that they are interested in you (Passage 2).\n3. Smiling: If your coworker smiles at you excessively, even when there is no apparent reason to do so, it may be a sign of flirtation (Passage 2).\n4. Seeking contact: If your coworker consistently finds reasons to be near you or seek you out discreetly, it may indicate that they have feelings for you (Passage 3).\n\nHowever, it is essential to remember that these signs alone do not necessarily mean that your coworker is interested in you romantically. It's important to pay attention to the context of their behavior and communicate openly to avoid misunderstandings.\n\nIf you are unsure about your coworker's intentions, it may be best to avoid physical contact and observe their behavior over time before making any conclusions. Additionally, it's crucial to respect your coworker's boundaries and maintain a professional work environment.", "pred": "According to the passages, you can tell if your male coworker is flirting with you if he finds any reason to touch you, as this is described as \"a little obnoxious\" if he doesn't like you. Additionally, if he constantly seeks you out discreetly and is always at your desk, it's a guarantee that he likes you. However, the passages do not provide specific information on how to differentiate between general friendliness and actual flirting in the case of a male coworker.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: According to the passages, you can tell if your male coworker is flirting with you if he finds any reason to touch you, as this is described as \"a little obnoxious\" if he doesn't like you. Additionally, if he constantly seeks you out discreetly and is always at your desk, it's a guarantee that he likes you. However, the passages do not provide specific information on how to differentiate between general friendliness and actual flirting in the case of a male coworker.", "time_sec": 9.067, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.62, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "74", "question": "how to tell if your male coworker is flirting with you", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are some signs that a male coworker may be flirting with you:\n\n* He finds reasons to be near you and seek you out discreetly (Passage 3)\n* He is always staring at you and catches your eye (Passage 2)\n* He touches you unnecessarily or finds excuses to have physical contact with you (Passage 2)\n* He smiles at you excessively or without apparent reason (Passage 2)\n* He seems uncomfortable or suggests that you not continue flirting with him (Passage 1)\n\nIf you observe these behaviors from a male coworker, it may be a sign that he is interested in you and flirting with you. However, it's essential to remember that these signs alone do not necessarily mean the person likes you romantically, and it's possible they just have a friendly interest in you. To confirm their intentions, consider observing their behavior over time, and if you feel comfortable, try initiating a conversation or asking them out.", "pred": "According to the passages, you can tell if your male coworker is flirting with you if he finds any reason to touch you, as this is described as \"a little obnoxious\" if he doesn't like you. Additionally, if he constantly seeks you out discreetly and is always at your desk, it's a guarantee that he likes you. However, the passages do not provide specific information on how to differentiate between general friendliness and actual flirting in the case of a male coworker.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: According to the passages, you can tell if your male coworker is flirting with you if he finds any reason to touch you, as this is described as \"a little obnoxious\" if he doesn't like you. Additionally, if he constantly seeks you out discreetly and is always at your desk, it's a guarantee that he likes you. However, the passages do not provide specific information on how to differentiate between general friendliness and actual flirting in the case of a male coworker.", "time_sec": 9.173, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.37, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "75", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the passages provided, the steps to cook brats are as follows:\n\n1. Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook. \n2. Combine beer and onions in a large pot and bring it to a boil. Submerge the bratwurst in the beer, adding red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper.\n3. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes.\n4. In the meantime, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate.\n5. After simmering, remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and reduce the heat to low, continuing to cook the onions.\n6. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill, turning occasionally, for about 10 to 14 minutes until they are browned evenly.\n7. Serve the bratwurst with the beer and onion mixture as a topping or side.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "time_sec": 13.707, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 63.41, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "77", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "To cook brats, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat.\n2. Lightly oil the grate.\n3. Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook.\n4. Place the bratwurst in a large pot with onions, butter, and beer.\n5. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes.\n6. Remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and continue cooking on the preheated grill for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally.\n7. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "time_sec": 13.641, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 66.67, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "78", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to cook brats:\n\nStep 1: Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat.\n\nStep 2: Lightly oil the grate.\n\nStep 3: Combine beer and onions in a large pot, bring to a boil, and submerge the bratwurst. Add red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper.\n\nStep 4: Simmer the bratwurst in the beer mixture for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly.\n\nStep 5: Remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and grill it on the preheated grill for an additional 5 to 10 minutes, turning occasionally, until browned and cooked through.\n\nStep 6: Serve the bratwurst with the beer mixture as a topping or side dish.\n\nNote: It's important to prick the bratwurst with a fork before cooking to prevent them from exploding while cooking. Also, cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for up to 5 days or frozen for up to 3 months.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "time_sec": 13.659, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.67, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "79", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief guide on how to cook brats:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat.\n2. Lightly oil the grate.\n3. Combine beer, onions, red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper in a large pot. Bring to a boil.\n4. Submerge the bratwurst in the beer mixture.\n5. Reduce heat to medium and cook for 10 to 12 minutes.\n6. Remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and place them on the preheated grill.\n7. Cook the bratwurst for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once.\n8. Serve the bratwurst with the beer mixture as a topping or side.\n\nSo, to cook brats, you first prepare the grill, then cook the bratwurst in a beer mixture before grilling them until they're done.", "pred": "To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook bratwurst, first prick them with a fork to prevent exploding. Place them in a large stock pot with onions, butter, and beer. Bring the mixture to a boil, then reduce heat to medium and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat, lightly oil the grate, and cook the bratwurst for 10 to 14 minutes, turning occasionally to brown evenly. Alternatively, after simmering, cook the bratwurst on a preheated grill for 5 to 10 minutes, turning once. Serve with the beer mixture as a topping or side. Cooked bratwurst can be stored in the fridge for about 5 days or in the freezer for up to 3 months.", "time_sec": 13.651, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.25, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "80", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is unknown. However, it is believed to be a result of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors may include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (either too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include diarrhea, constipation, or alternating between the two, belly pains or cramps that worsen after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, as well as a lot of gas or bloating.", "pred": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is a type of IBS characterized by diarrhea. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.", "time_sec": 14.015, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 78.86, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
