{"id": "89", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here are the steps to prepare a blood slide:\n\n1. Sterilize the needle with a flame before using it to prick your finger or obtain blood from another source.\n2. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n3. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n4. Hold another clean slide at an angle of about 45 degrees and gently touch the blood with one end of the slide, allowing the blood to run along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n5. Carefully push the slide along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n6. Repeat step 4 and 5 to make two smears.\n7. Allow the slides to air dry.\n8. Label the slides clearly once they are dry.\n9. Place the slides in provided slide transport containers.\n\nNote: It is important to avoid coming into contact with another person's blood, and to wear rubber gloves if it is unavoidable.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.", "time_sec": 12.256, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 70.94, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "90", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\n\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so that it runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n4. Push the second slide carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n5. Repeat step 4 to make two smears.\n6. Allow the smears to air dry.\n7. Label the slides clearly.\n8. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.\n\nNote: It is important to sterilize the needle used to obtain the blood sample by heating it with a flame before using it. Additionally, it is essential to wear rubber gloves when handling blood to avoid coming into contact with potentially infectious materials.", "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To prepare a blood slide, first sterilize a needle with a flame. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end. Take another clean slide, hold it at a 45-degree angle, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. Make two smears, allow them to air dry, and label clearly. Once dried, place the slides in provided slide transport containers. Always avoid contact with another person's blood and wear rubber gloves if unavoidable, as blood can carry diseases that can be transferred from person to person.", "time_sec": 12.49, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 71.88, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "91", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are ones that the body cannot produce on its own, thus they must be obtained through diet. In adults, there are eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. For infants, histidine is also considered essential. On the other hand, nonessential amino acids are those that the body can produce, even if they are not present in the diet. These include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. While not essential to obtain from diet, these amino acids are still necessary as they are used by the body to build proteins needed for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "time_sec": 5.409, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.84, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "92", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Some examples of nonessential amino acids are alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "time_sec": 5.036, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.52, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "94", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between essential and non-essential amino acids are:\n\nEssential Amino Acids:\n\n* Must be obtained from the diet\n* Cannot be produced by the body\n* Nine out of 20 amino acids are considered essential (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine for infants)\n\nNon-Essential Amino Acids:\n\n* Can be produced by the body\n* Not necessarily required to obtain from the diet\n* Include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine\n\nIt is important to note that even though non-essential amino acids can be produced by the body, it is still important to consume a balanced diet that includes all of the essential amino acids to ensure proper bodily functions.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "time_sec": 5.063, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.27, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "95", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nEssential amino acids are those that cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n\nPassage 1 states that nine amino acids are essential for humans, but only eight of them are essential for adults (histidine is only essential for infants). Passage 2 explains that nonessential amino acids are not unnecessary, as the body can produce them from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 3 reiterates that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be produced and must be obtained from the diet.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products, so they are not required to be obtained from the diet.", "time_sec": 5.063, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "96", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "The typical weather in Indiana is characterized by long, warm, humid, and wet summers. The winters are chilly and windy with some snowfall. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F. The state also experiences some rainfall, with the wettest year recording 57.65 inches of rain and the driest 24.97 inches. The most snowfall recorded in a season was 74.5 inches.", "pred": "The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.", "time_sec": 5.981, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.32, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "97", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid, and wet summers, and chilly and windy winters. The temperature ranges from 22°F to 85°F throughout the year. The passages also mention snowfall and snow accumulation in specific cities, but do not provide a comprehensive overview of the typical weather in Indiana. Therefore, it is not possible to provide a complete answer based solely on the given passages.", "pred": "The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The typical weather in Indiana features long, warm, humid, and wet summers and chilly, windy winters, with temperatures ranging from 22°F to 85°F annually, rarely dropping below 3°F or exceeding 92°F, and partly cloudy conditions year-round.", "time_sec": 5.921, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.84, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "100", "question": "how to sync audiobook from iphone to itunes", "gt": "To sync an audiobook from iPhone to iTunes, follow these steps:\n\n1. Open iTunes on your computer.\n2. Turn on the Manually manage music setting in iTunes.\n3. Download the audiobook from iTunes onto your iPhone.\n4. Connect your iPhone to the USB cable and plug the other end of the USB cable into a USB port on the computer.\n5. Wait for iTunes to sync the audiobook to your iPhone.\n6. After the sync is complete, the audiobook will be on your iPhone.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.667, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "101", "question": "how to sync audiobook from iphone to itunes", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to sync audiobooks from an iPhone to iTunes:\n\n1. Turn on the Manually manage music setting in iTunes.\n2. Download the audiobook you want from iTunes.\n3. Connect your iPhone to the computer via USB cable.\n4. Click on \"Audiobooks\" on the left task pane of the iTunes window.\n5. Select the audiobook you want to sync by clicking on its title.\n6. Click \"Sync Audiobooks\" and choose the audiobooks you want to transfer.\n7. Click \"Apply\" to start the syncing process.\n\nAlternatively, you can use Syncios Audiobook Transfer to transfer audiobooks from your iPhone to iTunes. The software allows two-way transfer of files, so you can not only backup files to your PC but also transfer audiobooks from your PC to your iPhone. Here are the steps to use Syncios Audiobook Transfer:\n\n1. Launch Syncios Audiobook Transfer on your computer.\n2. Connect your iPhone to the computer via USB cable.\n3. Click on \"Audiobooks\" on the top menu bar.\n4. Select the audiobooks you want to transfer and click \"Export\" to save them to your computer.\n5. Alternatively, you can also click \"Import\" to add new audiobooks from your computer to your iPhone.\n6. Click \"Apply\" to start the transfer process.\n\nNote that the above steps are based on the assumption that you have already turned on the Manually manage music setting in iTunes. If you have not done so, you will need to do so before attempting to sync your audiobooks.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.625, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.16, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "102", "question": "how to sync audiobook from iphone to itunes", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to sync audiobooks from iPhone to iTunes:\n\n1. Connect your iPhone to your computer using a USB cable.\n2. Open iTunes on your computer.\n3. Click on the \"Audiobooks\" tab on the left side of the iTunes window.\n4. Check the boxes next to the audiobooks you want to sync to your iPhone.\n5. Click the \"Sync Audiobooks\" button and choose the audiobooks you want to transfer or select \"All Audiobooks\" to transfer all of them to your iPhone.\n6. Click \"Apply\" at the bottom of the screen to finish the syncing process.\n\nAlternatively, you can use Syncios Audiobook Transfer to transfer audiobooks from your computer to your iOS device. To do this, follow these steps:\n\n1. Launch Syncios Audiobook Transfer on your computer.\n2. Connect your iPhone to your computer using a USB cable.\n3. Click on the \"Books\" tab on the top menu bar.\n4. Select the audiobooks you want to transfer from your computer to your iPhone.\n5. Click the \"Import\" button to transfer the selected audiobooks to your iPhone.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.713, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.14, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "103", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "The various systems in the human body work together to ensure its normal functioning. For instance, the urinary system works with all other systems to clean the blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. This includes organs like the kidneys and lower intestines. Similarly, the respiratory system also collaborates with all other systems to provide oxygen to all body parts and remove carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. The respiratory system comprises the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "pred": "The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "time_sec": 7.533, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 69.03, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "104", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "The body systems work together in order to maintain the normal functioning of the human body. These systems include the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous systems. Each system has its own specific functions, but they also work in conjunction with one another to ensure the overall well-being of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from various systems such as the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems.", "pred": "The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "time_sec": 7.695, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.38, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "105", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how the body systems work together:\n\n1. The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, and nervous systems all work together to help keep the body functioning normally. (Passage 1)\n2. The urinary system works closely with all other systems of the body to clean blood from the body and remove waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. (Passage 2)\n3. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the body systems work together in the following ways:\n\n* The skeletal system provides support and structure for the body, while the muscular system helps move the body and its parts.\n* The digestive system breaks down food into nutrients that can be absorbed and used by the body, while the respiratory system provides oxygen for energy production.\n* The circulatory system delivers oxygenated blood to the body's cells and removes carbon dioxide, while the urinary system removes waste products from the blood.\n* The endocrine system produces hormones that regulate various bodily functions, including growth and development, metabolism, and reproductive processes.\n* The lymphatic system helps defend the body against infection and disease, while the immune system fights off foreign invaders.\n* The nervous system controls and coordinates the body's functions, including movement, sensation, and thought processes.\n\nOverall, the body's systems work together in a complex interplay to maintain homeostasis and ensure the proper functioning of the body.", "pred": "The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "time_sec": 7.479, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.03, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "106", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the body systems work together as follows:\n\nThe skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, and reproductive systems all work together to help keep the body functioning normally. The urinary system helps to clean waste products from the other systems, while the respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the other systems. The nervous system plays a crucial role in coordinating the functions of all the other systems.", "pred": "The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The body systems work together in several ways. The urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems, including the kidneys and lower intestines. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems, including the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "time_sec": 7.479, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.24, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "107", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "gt": "The Windward and Leeward Islands are part of the Lesser Antilles of the Caribbean Sea. The Windward Islands are in the southeast and are exposed to the northeast trade winds, hence they receive more rain and are wetter. On the other hand, the Leeward Islands are located in the northwest and are away from the wind, making them drier.", "pred": "The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "time_sec": 5.223, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.83, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "108", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "gt": "The Windward Islands are exposed to the wind of the northeast trade winds and are located on the southeastern side of the Caribbean Sea. The Leeward Islands, on the other hand, are located on the northwestern side of the Lesser Antilles and are away from the wind. The windward side receives more rain and is wetter, while the leeward side is drier but still receives some rain.", "pred": "The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "time_sec": 5.361, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.39, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "109", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the Windward Islands are located in the southeastern part of the Caribbean Sea and are exposed to the wind (\"windward\") of the northeast trade winds (northeasterlies), while the Leeward Islands are located in the northwestern part of the Lesser Antilles and are away from the wind (\"lee\"). The main difference between them is their exposure to the wind and the resulting climate. The Windward Islands get more rain and are wetter due to their exposure to the wind, while the Leeward Islands are drier but still get rain.", "pred": "The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "time_sec": 5.301, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.59, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "111", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main difference between the Leeward and Windward Islands is their exposure to the wind and precipitation. The Windward Islands are exposed to the northeasterly trade winds and receive more rainfall, making them wetter and more humid, while the Leeward Islands are located on the leeward side of the mountains and receive less rainfall, making them drier. Therefore, the answer to the question is:\n\nThe Windward Islands are wetter and more humid due to their exposure to the northeasterly trade winds, while the Leeward Islands are drier and receive less rainfall.", "pred": "The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "time_sec": 5.402, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.33, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "112", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main difference between the Windward Islands and the Leeward Islands is their exposure to the northeast trade winds (northeasterlies). The Windward Islands are exposed to the wind and are therefore wetter and receive more rainfall, while the Leeward Islands are away from the wind and are drier, but still receive some rainfall. Additionally, the Windward Islands are located southeast of the Caribbean Sea, while the Leeward Islands are located northwest of the Caribbean Sea.", "pred": "The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The difference between the leeward and windward islands is that windward islands get more rain, are wetter, and are the side of the mountain closest to the ocean, while leeward islands are farther from shore, drier, and still receive some rain.", "time_sec": 5.299, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.84, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "113", "question": "how are water shares transferred", "gt": "To transfer water shares, the seller must return the Water Stock Certificate that was assigned to them and sign the back of it. The new owner of the property must provide proof of ownership. A fee of $75.00 must be paid to process a Water Stock Certificate transfer. After these steps, a new Water Stock Certificate will be issued to the new owner. If the original certificate is lost, an additional fee of $75.00 must be paid when filing a Lost Certificate Affidavit form, which is furnished by the Company and must be signed and notarized.", "pred": "Water shares are transferred by returning the assigned Water Stock Certificate, signing the back of the certificate, providing proof of ownership by the new owner, and paying a $75.00 fee to process the transfer.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Water shares are transferred by returning the assigned Water Stock Certificate, signing the back of the certificate, providing proof of ownership by the new owner, and paying a $75.00 fee to process the transfer.", "time_sec": 4.53, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.55, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
