{"id": "130", "question": "difference between vietnam and india", "gt": "The passages suggest that trends in India are more typical than those in Vietnam, particularly in the area of education. Vietnam's education system is described as relatively equitable, where poorer children receive the same treatment as their better-off counterparts. In contrast, India's early focus was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, and training teachers. Politically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. Both countries have a history of colonialism, with India being a former colony of the United Kingdom and Vietnam of France. Post-colonization, India experienced division and conflict among Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs. Cultural and economic links between India and Vietnam date back to the 2nd century.", "pred": "India and Vietnam differ in their education systems and historical relationships. In terms of education, India focused on establishing schools and training teachers to get children into school, while Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, providing poorer children with the same opportunities as better-off children. Historically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France, opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, and supported the unification of Vietnam. The two countries also share cultural and economic links dating back to the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: India and Vietnam differ in their education systems and historical relationships. In terms of education, India focused on establishing schools and training teachers to get children into school, while Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, providing poorer children with the same opportunities as better-off children. Historically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France, opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War, and supported the unification of Vietnam. The two countries also share cultural and economic links dating back to the 2nd century, with the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom influencing Vietnamese music.", "time_sec": 9.447, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.42, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "132", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Preheat your oven to 400°F (205° C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices if you like. Roasting time is dependent on the size of the cloves and may take up to 45 minutes or an hour for larger cloves. Once roasted, the garlic cloves can be stored in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.737, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "133", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "To roast individual garlic cloves, preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the garlic bulb, leaving the skins of the cloves intact. Cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up, and drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, rubbing it over the cloves. Roast the cloves in the preheated oven for about 45 minutes to an hour, or until they are softened. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.682, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "135", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Here are brief answers to the question on how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nPassage 1: The passage does not provide information on how to roast individual garlic cloves specifically. However, it does mention that larger garlic cloves may take up to an hour to soften when roasted, so it is possible to assume that smaller cloves will take less time.\n\nPassage 2: This passage provides some options for seasoning the garlic before roasting, but it does not provide instructions on how to roast individual garlic cloves.\n\nPassage 3: This passage provides a method for roasting entire garlic heads, but it does not address how to roast individual garlic cloves. The passage does mention that cutting the top of the garlic bulb and exposing the individual cloves can help them roast more evenly, but it does not provide specific instructions on how to do this.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.797, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.7, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "136", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "Sure! Here's how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nTo roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of each clove, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices, if desired.\n7. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for up to 45 minutes or until the cloves are softened to your liking.\n8. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nNote that the roasting time may vary depending on the size of your garlic cloves, so check on them periodically to avoid overcooking.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.64, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 2.94, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "137", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "gt": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the preheated oven for 45 minutes to an hour, or until the cloves are softened.\n7. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nYou can also season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices before roasting.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.6, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "138", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded as per section 43(6). This is done under the block concept, where all assets of the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are considered as a single asset. Any changes to the block's value must abide by the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961. Additionally, the assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset, even if it's not used for the purposes of business or profession in that year and subsequent years.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "time_sec": 5.029, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.42, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "139", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the given passages, when an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. The block concept is followed, where assets of the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are grouped together and considered as a single asset. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, even if it is not used for business or profession in that year and subsequent years.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "time_sec": 4.986, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.74, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "140", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, the Income Tax Act allows the scrap value of the asset to be deducted from the opening work-in-progress (WDV) of such asset in the previous year in which it was discarded. This reduction is taken into account while computing the depreciation expense for the subsequent years. However, any changes made to the value of the block must be made strictly in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of Income Tax Act 1961.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "time_sec": 5.081, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.1, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "141", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nAccording to passage 1, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This reduction is allowed under section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act.\n\nPassage 3 further states that the assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even though such discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in such year and subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the block value of the asset by the scrap value in the previous year in which it is discarded, and to claim depreciation on the residual value of the asset in the year of discard and subsequent years.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "time_sec": 5.059, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 37.4, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "142", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the treatment for an asset that is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nThe scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per Section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act. The block concept applies to all assets falling within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation, and any alterations to the value of the block must be made in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act. The assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even if the asset is not used for business or professional purposes in that year or subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment for an asset that is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the scrap value from the block in the previous year and claim depreciation on the residual value.", "pred": "As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: As per the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded.", "time_sec": 5.184, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.6, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "144", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Place the zipper gusset to the zipper with right sides facing each other, 1/4″ away from the center, and secure it with a zipper foot, sewing a line near the seam line. Then, place the zipper gusset lining on top with right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2″, then change the presser foot back to normal and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Place the zipper gusset to the zipper with right sides facing each other, 1/4″ away from the center, and secure it with a zipper foot, sewing a line near the seam line. Then, place the zipper gusset lining on top with right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2″, then change the presser foot back to normal and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together.", "time_sec": 14.135, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "146", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to sew a zipper gusset:\n\n1. Make a casing in the side seams by stitching the outer edge of the gusset and the back side seam.\n2. Slide the boning right down into the casing.\n3. Push the seam allowance to the back and tack the casing to the lining, if necessary, so it doesn't move.\n4. Make a zipper sandwich by placing one piece of lining fabric face up, followed by the zipper, and then the outer fabric right side facing down.\n5. Sew across the top of the zipper sandwich with a zipper foot, making sure to line up the three edges.\n6. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper.\n7. Trace all pattern pieces to the remaining fabric and cut them out.\n8. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, with the right sides facing each other, and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position.\n9. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of the zipper gusset, with the right sides facing down, and stitch along the seam line.\n10. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line.\n11. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset.\n12. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2\".\n13. Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch along the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabrics together nicely.\n\nTherefore, to sew a zipper gusset, you need to make a casing in the side seams, insert the boning, make a zipper sandwich, sew the zipper gusset to the zipper, and attach the lining to the zipper gusset.", "pred": "To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Place the zipper gusset to the zipper with right sides facing each other, 1/4″ away from the center, and secure it with a zipper foot, sewing a line near the seam line. Then, place the zipper gusset lining on top with right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2″, then change the presser foot back to normal and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To sew a zipper gusset, first trace and cut the pattern. Place the zipper gusset to the zipper with right sides facing each other, 1/4″ away from the center, and secure it with a zipper foot, sewing a line near the seam line. Then, place the zipper gusset lining on top with right side facing down and stitch on the seam line. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line. Repeat for the other side of the zipper gusset. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2″, then change the presser foot back to normal and stitch on the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabric together.", "time_sec": 14.176, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.3, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "150", "question": "how to bake hard boiled egg", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to bake hard-boiled eggs:\n\n1. Place cold eggs in a single layer in a pan with enough cold water to cover them by about 1 inch.\n2. Place the pan on the stove over high heat.\n3. As soon as the water comes to a boil, turn the heat off, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat.\n4. Allow the eggs to sit in the water for the appropriate amount of time, depending on the size of the eggs (9 minutes for medium, 12 minutes for large, and 15 minutes for extra-large).\n\nNote: Do not peek during the cooking process, and do not worry if the eggs are not perfectly symmetrical or have a slightly greenish-gray yolk.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.629, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 7.14, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "158", "question": "how to factory reset a htc one vx", "gt": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, you can follow the steps mentioned in passage 3. Slide the Notifications panel open, tap on Storage, then Factory data reset, and finally tap on Reset phone and Erase everything.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.865, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "159", "question": "how to factory reset a htc one vx", "gt": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Switch off the device by holding down the Power button for a while.\n2. Press and hold the Volume Down + Power key for a couple of seconds until Bootloader Mode menu appears on the screen.\n3. From the menu, choose \"Factory Data Reset\" by using Volume buttons to scroll and Power key to select.\n4. Confirm the reset by pressing \"Erase Everything\".\n5. Release all keys when the process is complete.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 1.637, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "162", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "gt": "PSA, or Prostate-specific antigen, is a protein produced by cells of the prostate gland. The level of PSA in a man's blood is measured by the PSA test, which involves sending a blood sample to a laboratory for analysis. The results are usually reported as nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate conditions such as prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition like prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer, benign prostate hyperplasia, or inflammation of the prostate.", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.", "time_sec": 9.548, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 76.36, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "166", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "gt": "Based on the given passages, PSA levels refer to the amount of prostate-specific antigen present in a man's blood. The level of PSA is measured in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Normal PSA levels range between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml, and a moderate elevation in PSA levels may require further testing, such as a free PSA test, to determine the ratio of free to total PSA and help distinguish between prostate cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA. Therefore, the PSA test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer and to detect elevated PSA levels that may indicate prostate cancer or other prostate issues.", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in a man's blood, measured in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer (about a 25% chance), benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate.", "time_sec": 9.476, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.74, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "167", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "To create a graph for your research, you first need to collect all of your data, remembering that the graph should show a comparison among categories. If you are creating a bar graph, you will use rectangular bars to represent different values. You need to title your graph and determine how high your graph needs to go. If your data points are substantially different in scale, you can use a pie chart. The y-axis of your graph will have a scale, and each bar’s height should be proportionate to the size of the category it represents. Make sure to consider any disproportionate responses that might impact the scale of your graph. Regardless of the type of graph you choose, it should provide a clear visual representation of your information.", "pred": "To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \n1. **Collect data** relevant to your research question (e.g., \"how many inches of rainfall a city received between February 2005 and February 2006\").  \n2. **Title the graph** and determine the scale for the y-axis (e.g., \"inches of rainfall\").  \n3. **Ensure bars are proportional** to the category values (e.g., height of each bar matches rainfall amount for a month).  \n4. **Use graph paper** to create the visual representation, as it helps in accurately plotting data and adding color for clarity.  \n5. **Compare categories** (e.g., months) using rectangular bars, where each bar’s height corresponds to the measured value.  \n\n*Note: The passages emphasize that bar graphs are ideal for comparing categories (e.g., rainfall across months), and graph paper aids in creating a \"great graph.\"*  \n\nAnswer: To make a bar graph, collect data on your research topic (e.g., rainfall in inches for specific months), title the graph, set the y-axis scale, and plot proportional rectangular bars for each category (e", "time_sec": 20.269, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 10.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "169", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a bar graph for your research:\n\n1. Collect your data: The first step is to gather all the data you want to represent in your graph. Make sure the data is organized and easy to compare.\n2. Decide on the x-axis and y-axis: The x-axis typically represents the category or group being compared, while the y-axis represents the value or amount of each category.\n3. Determine the height of the graph: The height of the graph will depend on the highest value in your data set. Make sure to choose a height that allows for clear representation of all the data points.\n4. Create the bars: Use rectangular bars to represent each category, making sure the height of each bar is proportional to the value it represents.\n5. Add labels and titles: Label each bar with its corresponding category and add a title to the graph that describes the data being represented.\n6. Consider using a pie chart: If you have only a few data points to display and each one is substantially different in scale, a pie chart might be a better option.\n7. Format and refine: Make sure the graph is visually appealing and easy to read. Adjust the scaling of the y-axis if needed, and consider adding colors or shading to make the graph more engaging.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to create a clear and effective bar graph for your research. Good luck!", "pred": "To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To make a bar graph for your research, follow these steps based on the passages:  \n1. **Collect data** relevant to your research question (e.g., \"how many inches of rainfall a city received between February 2005 and February 2006\").  \n2. **Title the graph** and determine the scale for the y-axis (e.g., \"inches of rainfall\").  \n3. **Ensure bars are proportional** to the category values (e.g., height of each bar matches rainfall amount for a month).  \n4. **Use graph paper** to create the visual representation, as it helps in accurately plotting data and adding color for clarity.  \n5. **Compare categories** (e.g., months) using rectangular bars, where each bar’s height corresponds to the measured value.  \n\n*Note: The passages emphasize that bar graphs are ideal for comparing categories (e.g., rainfall across months), and graph paper aids in creating a \"great graph.\"*  \n\nAnswer: To make a bar graph, collect data on your research topic (e.g., rainfall in inches for specific months), title the graph, set the y-axis scale, and plot proportional rectangular bars for each category (e", "time_sec": 23.948, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.89, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "170", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "To soften brown sugar, you can place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Then put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil and cover tightly. Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days. To make hardened granulated sugar soft again, preheat an oven to its lowest temperature, 150-200 degrees. Remove the sugar from its package and put it in an ovenproof container. Place it in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes. Tap the sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn the oven off and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "time_sec": 12.943, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 73.89, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "172", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "To soften sugar, place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil. Cover tightly. Remove damp paper towel in a couple of days. Alternatively, preheat oven to lowest temperature (150-200 degrees) and remove sugar from package. Put in an ovenproof container and place in the warm oven for 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container and confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location.", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "time_sec": 13.026, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 71.05, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "173", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to soften sugar:\n\n1. To soften brown sugar, place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, and cover tightly. Remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days. (Passage 1)\n2. To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to a low temperature (150-200°F), remove the sugar from its package, and place it in an ovenproof container. Warm the sugar in the oven for about 15 minutes, then tap it with a spoon. If it starts to fall apart, leave it in the oven for a longer period of time to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container. (Passage 2)\n3. To keep brown sugar soft, pack it into the smallest container possible to minimize air exposure. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to soften other types of sugar, such as confectioner's sugar.", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "time_sec": 12.802, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.76, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "175", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to soften sugar:\n\n1. Place brown sugar in an airtight container with a damp paper towel or napkin on top. Cover tightly and let it sit for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel, and the sugar should be soft. (Passage 1)\n2. Preheat the oven to a low temperature (150-200 degrees), place hardened granulated sugar in an ovenproof container, and tap it with a spoon after 15 minutes. If it starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it in for an hour or two to dry out completely. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container. (Passage 2)\n3. Keep brown sugar soft by storing it in an airtight container with minimal air space between the sugar and the lid. Pack the sugar into the smallest container possible. (Passage 3)", "pred": "To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees), place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and warm it for approximately 15 minutes. If the sugar begins to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it inside for an hour or two to dry completely. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container to prevent hardening. For brown sugar, keep it sealed in an airtight container with minimal air exposure, using a small container to limit air space between the sugar and the lid, and place a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the sugar covered with plastic wrap or foil, then cover tightly; remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days.", "time_sec": 12.815, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 69.41, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "179", "question": "how to change the sleep time on my computer", "gt": "Sure, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to change the sleep time on my computer\" based on the given passages:\n\nTo change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click the Start menu and select Control Panel.\n2. In the Control Panel, click on Power Options.\n3. In the Power Options window, click on \"Change when the computer sleeps\" on the left side of the window.\n4. In the \"Put the computer to sleep\" section, change the duration of time to the desired amount of time you want the computer to sit idle before it goes to sleep.\n5. Alternatively, you can also toggle off the option to enter Sleep mode automatically.\n\nIf you are using Windows 10, you can also search for \"Power Options\" in the taskbar search box and select it to open the Power Options window.\n\nIf you are using Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1, you can move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen, right-click the Start button, and select Shut down or sign out, and then select Shut down.", "pred": "To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps based on the provided passages:  \n\n1. **For Windows 7**:  \n   - Click the Start Menu, select Control Panel, then open Power Management Settings.  \n   - Click \"Change Power Management settings\" in the Power Options window.  \n   - On the left side, select \"Change when the computer sleeps.\"  \n   - Adjust the \"Put the computer to sleep\" entry to your desired idle time (e.g., 10 minutes, 30 minutes) or disable sleep mode entirely.  \n\n2. **For Windows 10**:  \n   - Click the Start Menu, type \"power options\" in the taskbar search box, and select \"Power Options.\"  \n   - Follow the on-screen instructions to modify the sleep time under \"Sleep\" settings.  \n\n3. **For Windows 8.1/Windows RT 8.1**:  \n   - Select the Start button, then choose Power > Shut down (this is for shutting down, not sleep settings; sleep settings may require navigating to Power Options via Control Panel).  \n\nNote: The passages specify steps for Windows 7, 10", "time_sec": 20.257, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.64, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "180", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "gt": "Hugging has significant psychological effects. It creates a warm feeling that affects the entire body, akin to the effect of many drugs operating simultaneously. This could be due to the feeling of a loving embrace, leading to good relationships and emotional intimacy. It also enhances empathy and listening skills, which can lead to a psychological hug. Furthermore, research has found that hugging can significantly reduce worries about mortality and boost self-esteem. This soothing effect can be achieved even when hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear.", "pred": "Psychological hugs, as described in the passages, are achieved through the use of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can lead to emotional intimacy, happy marriages, and make the conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a hug. Additionally, hugging, even with inanimate objects like teddy bears, has been shown to significantly reduce existential fears and worry of mortality, as evidenced by research published in the journal Psychological Science.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_vcd_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Psychological hugs, as described in the passages, are achieved through the use of empathy and listening skills (empathic listening), which can lead to emotional intimacy, happy marriages, and make the conversation partner feel as though they are receiving a hug. Additionally, hugging, even with inanimate objects like teddy bears, has been shown to significantly reduce existential fears and worry of mortality, as evidenced by research published in the journal Psychological Science.", "time_sec": 7.895, "has_vcd": false, "degraded_reason": "text-only: no visual to distort", "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "alpha": 0.1, "noise_std": 0.1}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.18, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
